  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "Determine the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2x+24y+21=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.",
  "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2x+24y+21 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 0+24y+21 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}+24y+21 & =0 \\\\ 4[{y}^{2}+6y]+21 & =0 \\\\ 4[(y+3)^{2}-9]+21 & =0 \\\\ 4(y+3)^{2}-36+21 & =0 \\\\ 4(y+3)^{2}-15 & =0 \\\\ 4(y+3)^{2} & =15 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{15}{4} \\\\ y+3 & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2} \\\\ y & =-3\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-3-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})$ and $(0,-3+\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).$",
